  meaningfully more code and more concepts to hold, for ergonomics you'd notice mostly in an editor with types, which
  this repo doesn't have. It also nudges toward eventually adopting zod outright, at which point the hand-rolled builder
  was wasted.

  [1mC. Rules declared in HTML attributes[0m

  [38;5;153m<input name="email" data-validate="required|email">[39m; the binder parses the markup and no JS schema exists. Genuinely
  appealing — rules live next to the field they govern, and adding a form means only writing HTML. But rules become
  stringly-typed with no way to catch a typo, cross-field [38;5;153mmatches[39m and custom functions don't express cleanly in an
  attribute, and the core stops being independently usable because the rules only exist in the DOM. That last point
  conflicts with the two-layer split you picked.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m It's the least code, the custom escape hatch falls out for free rather than being bolted on, and
  it keeps the core a pure function that's trivial to test. B is the same shape wearing a nicer coat; C trades away the
  layer separation you just asked for.

  One unavoidable side issue, whichever you pick: the repo has two module conventions — [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a
  bare browser script with globals — and no bundler. A module that both the browser and a test runner can import needs
  one convention. I'd standardize on ES modules, which means [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gains
  [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m, and [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m convert from [38;5;153mrequire[39m/[38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m to [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m — about four
  lines across two files that are otherwise unrelated to this work. I want that on the record before it happens rather
  than after. The alternative is naming the new files [38;5;153m.mjs[39m and leaving those two alone, but some static servers send
  [38;5;153m.mjs[39m with a MIME type browsers refuse to execute as modules, so it trades a known small edit for an
  environment-dependent failure.

